// std/trace.jtr — timed, nested segments of work, exported to whoever is listening.
//
// TIER: `std`. A `Tracer` reaches the operating system through exactly two things it is
// GIVEN — a `time.Clock` for the timestamps and an `Exporter` for the destination — so a
// tracer over `time.manual()` and a buffered exporter touches nothing at all, and every
// duration it reports is exactly what the test chose. That is `std/log`'s discipline, and
// this module is `std/log`'s shape with nesting and a stopwatch added.
//
// ─── The noun is "segment", and the reason is a collision ─────────────────────
//
// The word the rest of the world uses is "span", and it is taken three times in this tree:
// `std/http` has header spans, `std/diag` has source spans, and `@span` is the checked
// work-span attribute of the cost model. A fourth `Span` would be a type a reader could
// not grep for and a name that meant something different in every module that imported
// two of them. So the unit here is a **segment**: a named stretch of one trace with a
// start, a duration, a parent and attributes. The segment's id is a plain `i64`, and there
// is no `Segment` struct to hold — the tracer holds the open ones and the exporter is
// handed the finished ones.
//
// ─── No hidden tracer ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// There is no `trace.begin("x")` free function, no ambient default, and no initialization
// on first use. A service is HANDED a `Tracer` the way it is handed a `Logger` and a
// `metrics.Registry`, and the clock and the exporter are visible in the signature that
// made it. A global tracer is a hidden global with a clock and an output attached, and
// this tree has spent a lot of effort not having hidden globals.
//
// ─── The exporter is a vtable, not a trait ────────────────────────────────────
//
// An `Exporter` is a `*mut u8` context plus four function pointers — the `runtime.Task`
// and `mem.Allocator` shape. Not a trait, for the reason `std/sink`'s header gives:
// `@no_alloc` passes VACUOUSLY through a trait method, reporting success on code that
// allocates on every call, and an exporter is exactly the kind of thing that would. A
// fn pointer is at least honest about being opaque, and the state it carries is explicit.
//
// The four calls are a visitor over ONE finished segment:
//
//     on_open(ctx, id, parent, name, start, dur)
//     on_str(ctx, key, value)      ← once per string attribute, in the order they were set
//     on_i64(ctx, key, value)      ← once per integer attribute, likewise
//     on_close(ctx)
//
// The tracer drives them from `end`, while the segment's bytes are still in its own arena,
// so the exporter borrows and copies and owns nothing of the tracer's. Two exporters ship
// here — `to_text` (one logfmt-shaped line per segment) and `to_jsonl` (one JSON object
// per line) over the same `Out` buffer — plus `to_log`, which hands each finished segment
// to a `log.Logger` as one record. A test writes a fourth in a dozen lines, which is the
// property the vtable exists for.
//
// **Records arrive in END order, so a child precedes its parent.** A segment is exported
// when it finishes, and a parent finishes last. A consumer that wants the tree rebuilds it
// from `id` and `parent`; a consumer that only wants the durations does not have to.
//
// ─── Ids are a counter, and that is deliberate ────────────────────────────────
//
// Ids start at 1 and count up per tracer; `0` is "no parent". Under a manual clock two
// tracers running the same code produce the same ids and the same bytes, which is what
// lets `the_same_run_exports_identically_through_both_renderers` compare renderings
// rather than patterns. A process-unique or globally-unique id is a property of the
// SYSTEM the trace is shipped into, not of the code that produced it, so it is the
// exporter's job to add one if it wants one — the way a log shipper adds a host name.
//
// ─── Bounded, and every refusal is counted ────────────────────────────────────
//
// A tracer is sized ONCE at `make`: so many open segments, so many attributes, so many
// bytes of names and values. Nothing is allocated after that, and nothing grows. Opening
// past the limit is REFUSED — `begin` returns 0, which no real segment has — and counted,
// never silently truncated. The refusals are distinct because the fixes are:
//
//   dropped     `begin` refused: the open stack, the attribute table or the arena is full
//   truncated   an attribute refused: the table or the arena is full; the segment still
//               exports with the attributes that fit, and the caller saw `false`
//   abandoned   a segment still open when an OUTER one ended, or when the tracer was freed;
//               it is discarded, never exported — the early-`return` bug, made visible
//   unmatched   `end` named a segment that is not open at all
//
// `end(t, id)` names the segment on purpose. An `end` that closed "the innermost one"
// could never notice the early return between a child's `begin` and its `end`; naming the
// id is what makes the mis-nesting observable, and the outer segment still ends correctly.
//
// Attributes go on the INNERMOST open segment, and only there. A parent may set more after
// a child has ended — the demo sets `status` after its `db` segment closes — but not while
// one is open. That is what keeps the arena a stack: a segment's bytes and attributes are
// released when it ends, in LIFO order, and a tracer's memory is exactly its `make` size
// for its whole life.
//
// ─── What this does NOT do ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// No sampling, no cross-process propagation, no trace ids shared between tracers, no
// batching or asynchronous shipping, no wire protocol. Each is a policy a system chooses;
// none can be chosen here for every caller. `to_log` is the whole of the integration with
// `std/log`: a segment becomes one record, and this module does not become a logger.
//
// Worked example:  examples/std/trace_demo.jtr
// Run the tests:   jestyrc test examples/std/trace_test.jtr

import "sink"
import "json"
import "log"
import "time"

// ---- the exporter -----------------------------------------------------------------

// A visitor over one finished segment. `@copy`: a pointer and four function pointers,
// exactly like an `Allocator`.
@copy pub struct Exporter {
    ctx: *mut u8,
    on_open: fn(*mut u8, i64, i64, str, i64, i64),
    on_str: fn(*mut u8, str, str),
    on_i64: fn(*mut u8, str, i64),
    on_close: fn(*mut u8),
}

@no_alloc @no_os pub fn exporter(ctx: *mut u8,
                                 open_fn: fn(*mut u8, i64, i64, str, i64, i64),
                                 str_fn: fn(*mut u8, str, str),
                                 i64_fn: fn(*mut u8, str, i64),
                                 close_fn: fn(*mut u8)) -> Exporter {
    return Exporter{ ctx: ctx, on_open: open_fn, on_str: str_fn, on_i64: i64_fn, on_close: close_fn }
}

// The exporter that discards everything. For a tracer whose only job is the accounting,
// and for a test of the tracer that wants no exporter in the way. It is a real exporter
// rather than an `Option` so `Tracer` has a total value for the field.
fn drop_open(ctx: *mut u8, id: i64, parent: i64, name: str, start: i64, dur: i64) {}
fn drop_str(ctx: *mut u8, key: str, value: str) {}
fn drop_i64(ctx: *mut u8, key: str, value: i64) {}
fn drop_close(ctx: *mut u8) {}

@no_alloc @no_os pub fn discard() -> Exporter {
    return Exporter{ ctx: null, on_open: &drop_open, on_str: &drop_str, on_i64: &drop_i64, on_close: &drop_close }
}

// ---- the tracer -------------------------------------------------------------------

// One open segment is `SEG_STRIDE` words of the `meta` table:
//   id, start, name offset, name length, attribute-table index at open.
const SEG_STRIDE: usize = 5
// One attribute is `ATTR_STRIDE` words of the `attrs` table:
//   kind (ATTR_STR | ATTR_I64), key offset, key length, value offset | value, value length.
const ATTR_STRIDE: usize = 5
const ATTR_STR: i64 = 0
const ATTR_I64: i64 = 1

pub struct Tracer {
    clock: time.Clock,
    exp: Exporter,
    meta: *mut i64,
    cap: usize,          // open segments the stack can hold
    depth: usize,        // open segments right now
    attrs: *mut i64,
    acap: usize,         // attributes the table can hold, across every open segment
    atop: usize,         // attributes in use right now
    raw: *mut u8,
    bcap: usize,         // bytes of names, keys and string values
    top: usize,          // bytes in use right now
    next_id: i64,
    finished: i64,
    dropped: i64,
    truncated: i64,
    abandoned: i64,
    unmatched: i64,
}

// The defaults `make` sizes a tracer with. Sixteen levels of nesting is far more than a
// request has; sixty-four attributes across them and four kilobytes of text is generous
// for names and values; `make_sized` is there for the caller who knows better.
pub const TRACE_DEPTH_CAP: usize = 16
pub const TRACE_ATTR_CAP: usize = 64
pub const TRACE_ARENA_CAP: usize = 4096

// `take` for the clock, because the tracer STORES it: a Jestyr borrow is second-class and
// may not outlive the call. The same signature `log.make` and `runtime.make` carry, for
// the same reason. The exporter is `@copy`, so it is simply copied in.
pub fn make(take c: time.Clock, read e: Exporter) -> Tracer {
    return make_sized(c, e, TRACE_DEPTH_CAP, TRACE_ATTR_CAP, TRACE_ARENA_CAP)
}

pub fn make_sized(take c: time.Clock, read e: Exporter, depth_cap: usize, attr_cap: usize, arena_cap: usize) -> Tracer {
    var meta: *mut i64 = alloc(i64, depth_cap * SEG_STRIDE)
    var attrs: *mut i64 = alloc(i64, attr_cap * ATTR_STRIDE)
    var raw: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, arena_cap)
    return Tracer{ clock: c, exp: e, meta: meta, cap: depth_cap, depth: 0,
                   attrs: attrs, acap: attr_cap, atop: 0, raw: raw, bcap: arena_cap, top: 0,
                   next_id: 1, finished: 0, dropped: 0, truncated: 0, abandoned: 0, unmatched: 0 }
}

// Release the tables. **Every segment still open is abandoned and counted**, not exported:
// a segment that never ended has no duration to report, and inventing one at teardown
// would put a plausible-looking record in the stream for work that did not finish.
pub fn free(mut t: Tracer) {
    if t.meta == null { return }
    t.abandoned = t.abandoned + (t.depth as i64)
    t.depth = 0
    t.atop = 0
    t.top = 0
    free_ptr(t.meta as *mut u8)
    free_ptr(t.attrs as *mut u8)
    free_ptr(t.raw)
    t.meta = null
    t.attrs = null
    t.raw = null
}

// ---- the accounting ---------------------------------------------------------------

// Segments that ended and reached the exporter.
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn finished(read t: Tracer) -> i64 { return t.finished }
// `begin` calls refused because a table was full.
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn dropped(read t: Tracer) -> i64 { return t.dropped }
// Attributes refused because a table was full.
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn truncated(read t: Tracer) -> i64 { return t.truncated }
// Segments discarded because an outer one ended first, or the tracer was freed.
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn abandoned(read t: Tracer) -> i64 { return t.abandoned }
// `end` calls naming a segment that was not open.
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn unmatched(read t: Tracer) -> i64 { return t.unmatched }

// How many segments are open right now.
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn open_count(read t: Tracer) -> usize { return t.depth }

// The innermost open segment's id, or 0 when nothing is open — the one the next
// `attr_*` would land on.
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn current(read t: Tracer) -> i64 {
    if t.depth == 0 { return 0 }
    return seg_word(t, t.depth - 1, 0)
}

// Is `id` open, at any depth?
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn is_open(read t: Tracer, id: i64) -> bool {
    return index_of(t, id) >= 0
}

// The tracer OWNS its clock, so time is driven through the tracer — `log.advance` and
// `runtime.advance` make the same argument: a test that advanced its own copy would be
// advancing a clock nothing reads, and every duration would come out zero.
pub fn advance(mut t: Tracer, by: i64) -> bool {
    return time.advance(t.clock, by)
}

// What the next `begin` would record as its start.
pub fn now(mut t: Tracer) -> i64 {
    return time.read_nanos(t.clock)
}

// ---- the tables, by word ----------------------------------------------------------

@no_alloc @no_os fn seg_word(read t: Tracer, i: usize, k: usize) -> i64 {
    var v: i64 = 0
    unsafe { v = (t.meta + (i * SEG_STRIDE + k)).* }
    return v
}

@no_alloc @no_os fn set_seg_word(mut t: Tracer, i: usize, k: usize, v: i64) {
    unsafe { (t.meta + (i * SEG_STRIDE + k)).* = v }
}

@no_alloc @no_os fn attr_word(read t: Tracer, i: usize, k: usize) -> i64 {
    var v: i64 = 0
    unsafe { v = (t.attrs + (i * ATTR_STRIDE + k)).* }
    return v
}

@no_alloc @no_os fn set_attr_word(mut t: Tracer, i: usize, k: usize, v: i64) {
    unsafe { (t.attrs + (i * ATTR_STRIDE + k)).* = v }
}

// The stack index of open segment `id`, searched from the innermost, or -1.
@no_alloc @no_os fn index_of(read t: Tracer, id: i64) -> isize {
    var i: usize = t.depth
    for i > 0 {
        i = i - 1
        if seg_word(t, i, 0) == id { return i as isize }
    }
    return 0 - 1
}

// A view of `len` arena bytes at `off`. The arena is only ever written through `push_text`
// from a `str`, so it is valid UTF-8 by construction and `from_utf8` cannot trap.
@no_alloc @no_os fn arena_view(read t: Tracer, off: usize, len: usize) -> str {
    let all: []u8 = slice(u8, t.raw, t.bcap)
    let en: usize = off + len
    return from_utf8(all[off .. en])
}

// Copy `text` onto the arena's top. The caller has already checked it fits.
@no_alloc @no_os fn push_text(mut t: Tracer, read text: str) -> usize {
    let off: usize = t.top
    var all: []u8 = slice(u8, t.raw, t.bcap)
    for i in 0..text.len {
        all[off + i] = text[i] as u8
    }
    t.top = t.top + text.len
    return off
}

// ---- opening and closing ----------------------------------------------------------

// Open a segment named `name` under the innermost open one. Returns its id, or 0 when the
// tracer refused — and it refuses, rather than growing, when the open stack or the arena
// is full. Both refusals are `dropped`, because the fix is the same: size the tracer for
// the work.
//
// **A freed tracer refuses rather than writing through a null table.** Tracing after
// shutdown is an ordering bug, and the alternative is a crash somewhere far from the
// `free` that caused it. It is not counted: nothing was full, the tracer is simply gone.
pub fn begin(mut t: Tracer, read name: str) -> i64 {
    if t.meta == null { return 0 }
    if t.depth >= t.cap {
        t.dropped = t.dropped + 1
        return 0
    }
    if t.top + name.len > t.bcap {
        t.dropped = t.dropped + 1
        return 0
    }
    let id: i64 = t.next_id
    t.next_id = t.next_id + 1
    let start: i64 = time.read_nanos(t.clock)
    let off: usize = push_text(t, name)
    let i: usize = t.depth
    set_seg_word(t, i, 0, id)
    set_seg_word(t, i, 1, start)
    set_seg_word(t, i, 2, off as i64)
    set_seg_word(t, i, 3, name.len as i64)
    set_seg_word(t, i, 4, t.atop as i64)
    t.depth = t.depth + 1
    return id
}

// Room for one more attribute whose text needs `bytes` of arena? Counts the refusal.
@no_alloc @no_os fn attr_fits(mut t: Tracer, bytes: usize) -> bool {
    if t.atop >= t.acap or t.top + bytes > t.bcap {
        t.truncated = t.truncated + 1
        return false
    }
    return true
}

// A string attribute on the innermost open segment. False, and nothing recorded, when no
// segment is open or the tables are full — the latter counted as `truncated`.
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn attr_str(mut t: Tracer, read key: str, read value: str) -> bool {
    if t.meta == null or t.depth == 0 { return false }
    if attr_fits(t, key.len + value.len) == false { return false }
    let koff: usize = push_text(t, key)
    let voff: usize = push_text(t, value)
    let i: usize = t.atop
    set_attr_word(t, i, 0, ATTR_STR)
    set_attr_word(t, i, 1, koff as i64)
    set_attr_word(t, i, 2, key.len as i64)
    set_attr_word(t, i, 3, voff as i64)
    set_attr_word(t, i, 4, value.len as i64)
    t.atop = t.atop + 1
    return true
}

// An integer attribute on the innermost open segment. The value lives in the table, so
// only the key needs arena space.
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn attr_i64(mut t: Tracer, read key: str, value: i64) -> bool {
    if t.meta == null or t.depth == 0 { return false }
    if attr_fits(t, key.len) == false { return false }
    let koff: usize = push_text(t, key)
    let i: usize = t.atop
    set_attr_word(t, i, 0, ATTR_I64)
    set_attr_word(t, i, 1, koff as i64)
    set_attr_word(t, i, 2, key.len as i64)
    set_attr_word(t, i, 3, value)
    set_attr_word(t, i, 4, 0)
    t.atop = t.atop + 1
    return true
}

// Discard the innermost open segment without exporting it, releasing its bytes.
@no_alloc @no_os fn pop(mut t: Tracer) {
    let i: usize = t.depth - 1
    t.top = seg_word(t, i, 2) as usize
    t.atop = seg_word(t, i, 4) as usize
    t.depth = i
}

// End segment `id`: its duration is the clock's reading now minus its start, and it goes
// to the exporter with its parent's id and every attribute set on it.
//
// **`id` need not be the innermost.** Every segment opened inside it and not yet ended is
// ABANDONED — discarded unexported and counted — and then `id` ends normally. That is the
// shape of the realistic bug, an early `return` between a child's `begin` and its `end`,
// and leaving the child open would corrupt every segment after it: the next `begin` would
// nest under a child that was supposed to be gone. An `id` that is not open at all is
// `unmatched`, and nothing changes.
//
// Returns whether a segment was exported.
pub fn end(mut t: Tracer, id: i64) -> bool {
    if t.meta == null { return false }
    let ix: isize = index_of(t, id)
    if ix < 0 {
        t.unmatched = t.unmatched + 1
        return false
    }
    let i: usize = ix as usize
    for t.depth > i + 1 {
        pop(t)
        t.abandoned = t.abandoned + 1
    }
    let now: i64 = time.read_nanos(t.clock)
    let start: i64 = seg_word(t, i, 1)
    var parent: i64 = 0
    if i > 0 { parent = seg_word(t, i - 1, 0) }
    let e: Exporter = t.exp
    let name: str = arena_view(t, seg_word(t, i, 2) as usize, seg_word(t, i, 3) as usize)
    e.on_open(e.ctx, id, parent, name, start, now - start)
    var a: usize = seg_word(t, i, 4) as usize
    for a < t.atop {
        let key: str = arena_view(t, attr_word(t, a, 1) as usize, attr_word(t, a, 2) as usize)
        if attr_word(t, a, 0) == ATTR_STR {
            let value: str = arena_view(t, attr_word(t, a, 3) as usize, attr_word(t, a, 4) as usize)
            e.on_str(e.ctx, key, value)
        } else {
            e.on_i64(e.ctx, key, attr_word(t, a, 3))
        }
        a = a + 1
    }
    e.on_close(e.ctx)
    pop(t)
    t.finished = t.finished + 1
    return true
}

// ---- a buffered destination, rendered two ways ------------------------------------
//
// **The state is behind a pointer because that is what a fn pointer can carry.** An
// `Out` is the `memprof.Counting` shape: a handle over a heap block, so the exporter's
// `ctx` and the caller's handle name the same bytes, and `out_view` reads what the
// callbacks wrote.
//
// A record is built in its own scratch sink and copied to the output only when it is
// complete and fits — `std/log`'s rule that a truncated record is NOT emitted, because a
// half-written JSON object is unparseable and a half-written logfmt line silently loses
// its last fields. A record that does not fit is counted in `out_lost` and the output
// stays a whole number of whole lines.

struct OutState {
    fmt: i32,
    rec: Sink,
    rraw: *mut u8,
    rcap: usize,
    jw: json.Writer,
    s: Sink,
    raw: *mut u8,
    cap: usize,
    records: i64,
    lost: i64,
}

@copy pub struct Out {
    p: *mut OutState,
}

const OUT_TEXT: i32 = 0
const OUT_JSONL: i32 = 1

// The scratch a single record is built in. A segment's line is a name, five numbers and
// its attributes; a kilobyte holds any of them, and one that does not fit is counted.
pub const TRACE_RECORD_CAP: usize = 1024

// An output buffer of `cap` bytes. Hand it to `to_text` OR `to_jsonl` — one of the two,
// once — and read it back with `out_view`.
pub fn buffer(cap: usize) -> Out {
    var p: *mut OutState = alloc(OutState, 1)
    var rraw: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, TRACE_RECORD_CAP)
    var raw: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, cap)
    unsafe {
        p.* = OutState{ fmt: OUT_TEXT, rec: sink.new(), rraw: rraw, rcap: TRACE_RECORD_CAP, jw: json.writer(),
                        s: sink.new(), raw: raw, cap: cap, records: 0, lost: 0 }
    }
    return Out{ p: p }
}

pub fn free_buffer(mut o: Out) {
    if o.p == null { return }
    unsafe {
        free_ptr(o.p.*.rraw)
        free_ptr(o.p.*.raw)
    }
    free_ptr(o.p as *mut u8)
    o.p = null
}

// What has been exported so far, one line per record.
pub fn out_view(read o: Out) -> str {
    unsafe {
        let buf: []u8 = slice(u8, o.p.*.raw, o.p.*.cap)
        return sink.view(o.p.*.s, buf)
    }
}

// Records that landed whole.
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn out_records(read o: Out) -> i64 { unsafe { return o.p.*.records } }
// Records that did not fit — in the record scratch or in the output — and were withheld.
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn out_lost(read o: Out) -> i64 { unsafe { return o.p.*.lost } }

// Forget everything exported so far; the next record starts at byte 0.
pub fn out_reset(mut o: Out) {
    unsafe {
        sink.reset(o.p.*.s)
        o.p.*.records = 0
        o.p.*.lost = 0
    }
}

// One line per segment, logfmt-shaped:
//
//     id=2 parent=1 name=parse start=1000 dur=100 bytes=512
//
// A value that would break the line — a space, an `=`, a quote, a control byte, an empty
// string — is quoted by `std/log`'s rule and `std/json`'s escaper, so the tree has ONE
// answer to "what does a quoted logfmt value look like" and it is the one `log_test`
// already covers.
pub fn to_text(read o: Out) -> Exporter {
    unsafe { o.p.*.fmt = OUT_TEXT }
    return Exporter{ ctx: o.p as *mut u8, on_open: &out_open, on_str: &out_str, on_i64: &out_i64, on_close: &out_close }
}

// One JSON object per segment, one per line:
//
//     {"id":2,"parent":1,"name":"parse","start":1000,"dur":100,"bytes":512}
//
// The same keys in the same order as the text line, so the two renderings carry the same
// record and `the_same_run_exports_identically_through_both_renderers` can say so.
pub fn to_jsonl(read o: Out) -> Exporter {
    unsafe { o.p.*.fmt = OUT_JSONL }
    return Exporter{ ctx: o.p as *mut u8, on_open: &out_open, on_str: &out_str, on_i64: &out_i64, on_close: &out_close }
}

// A logfmt value: bare when unambiguous, otherwise a JSON string. `std/log`'s predicate
// decides, and a fresh `json.Writer` renders, so no separator state leaks in.
@no_alloc @no_os fn put_text_value(mut s: Sink, mut buf: []u8, read v: str) {
    if log.needs_quoting(v) == false {
        sink.put_str(s, buf, v)
        return
    }
    var q: json.Writer = json.writer()
    json.put_str(q, s, buf, v)
}

fn out_open(ctx: *mut u8, id: i64, parent: i64, name: str, start: i64, dur: i64) {
    var o: *mut OutState = ctx as *mut OutState
    unsafe {
        var buf: []u8 = slice(u8, o.*.rraw, o.*.rcap)
        sink.reset(o.*.rec)
        o.*.jw = json.writer()
        if o.*.fmt == OUT_JSONL {
            json.begin_object(o.*.jw, o.*.rec, buf)
            json.key(o.*.jw, o.*.rec, buf, "id")
            json.put_i64(o.*.jw, o.*.rec, buf, id)
            json.key(o.*.jw, o.*.rec, buf, "parent")
            json.put_i64(o.*.jw, o.*.rec, buf, parent)
            json.key(o.*.jw, o.*.rec, buf, "name")
            json.put_str(o.*.jw, o.*.rec, buf, name)
            json.key(o.*.jw, o.*.rec, buf, "start")
            json.put_i64(o.*.jw, o.*.rec, buf, start)
            json.key(o.*.jw, o.*.rec, buf, "dur")
            json.put_i64(o.*.jw, o.*.rec, buf, dur)
            return
        }
        sink.put_str(o.*.rec, buf, "id=")
        sink.put_i64(o.*.rec, buf, id)
        sink.put_str(o.*.rec, buf, " parent=")
        sink.put_i64(o.*.rec, buf, parent)
        sink.put_str(o.*.rec, buf, " name=")
        put_text_value(o.*.rec, buf, name)
        sink.put_str(o.*.rec, buf, " start=")
        sink.put_i64(o.*.rec, buf, start)
        sink.put_str(o.*.rec, buf, " dur=")
        sink.put_i64(o.*.rec, buf, dur)
    }
}

fn out_str(ctx: *mut u8, key: str, value: str) {
    var o: *mut OutState = ctx as *mut OutState
    unsafe {
        var buf: []u8 = slice(u8, o.*.rraw, o.*.rcap)
        if o.*.fmt == OUT_JSONL {
            json.key(o.*.jw, o.*.rec, buf, key)
            json.put_str(o.*.jw, o.*.rec, buf, value)
            return
        }
        sink.put(o.*.rec, buf, 32 as u8)
        sink.put_str(o.*.rec, buf, key)
        sink.put(o.*.rec, buf, 61 as u8)          // '='
        put_text_value(o.*.rec, buf, value)
    }
}

fn out_i64(ctx: *mut u8, key: str, value: i64) {
    var o: *mut OutState = ctx as *mut OutState
    unsafe {
        var buf: []u8 = slice(u8, o.*.rraw, o.*.rcap)
        if o.*.fmt == OUT_JSONL {
            json.key(o.*.jw, o.*.rec, buf, key)
            json.put_i64(o.*.jw, o.*.rec, buf, value)
            return
        }
        sink.put(o.*.rec, buf, 32 as u8)
        sink.put_str(o.*.rec, buf, key)
        sink.put(o.*.rec, buf, 61 as u8)
        sink.put_i64(o.*.rec, buf, value)
    }
}

// The record is complete: copy it to the output as one line, or withhold it whole.
fn out_close(ctx: *mut u8) {
    var o: *mut OutState = ctx as *mut OutState
    unsafe {
        var buf: []u8 = slice(u8, o.*.rraw, o.*.rcap)
        if o.*.fmt == OUT_JSONL { json.end_object(o.*.jw, o.*.rec, buf) }
        if sink.overflowed(o.*.rec) {
            o.*.lost = o.*.lost + 1
            return
        }
        let n: usize = sink.written(o.*.rec)
        var dst: []u8 = slice(u8, o.*.raw, o.*.cap)
        if sink.written(o.*.s) + n + 1 > dst.len {
            o.*.lost = o.*.lost + 1
            return
        }
        let line: str = sink.view(o.*.rec, buf)
        sink.put_str(o.*.s, dst, line)
        sink.put_nl(o.*.s, dst)
        o.*.records = o.*.records + 1
    }
}

// ---- a segment as a log record ----------------------------------------------------
//
// One finished segment becomes ONE `std/log` record: the name is the message, and `id`,
// `parent` and `dur` are its first three fields, followed by the segment's attributes.
// The logger renders it in whichever format and to whichever writer it was made with —
// this module does not choose, and does not become a second logger.
//
// **Two clocks, deliberately.** The record's `ts` is the LOGGER's clock and the
// segment's `dur` is the TRACER's; under `time.host()` both are the wall clock at the
// moment the segment ended, and under two manual clocks each says what its own test
// chose. `start` is not repeated in the record: a log is ordered by `ts` already, and a
// consumer that wants the segment's own timeline uses `to_text` or `to_jsonl`.

struct LogState {
    lg: log.Logger,
    level: i32,
    s: Sink,
    raw: *mut u8,
    cap: usize,
}

@copy pub struct LogOut {
    p: *mut LogState,
}

// Take a logger and give every finished segment to it at `level`. `cap` bytes are the
// DESTINATION for a `writer.to_buffer()` logger — read them with `log_view` — and unused
// scratch for a stream logger, which is `std/writer`'s convention.
pub fn to_log(take lg: log.Logger, level: i32, cap: usize) -> LogOut {
    var p: *mut LogState = alloc(LogState, 1)
    var raw: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, cap)
    unsafe { p.* = LogState{ lg: lg, level: level, s: sink.new(), raw: raw, cap: cap } }
    return LogOut{ p: p }
}

pub fn log_exporter(read lo: LogOut) -> Exporter {
    return Exporter{ ctx: lo.p as *mut u8, on_open: &log_open, on_str: &log_str, on_i64: &log_i64, on_close: &log_close }
}

// The logger's destination buffer, for a buffered logger.
pub fn log_view(read lo: LogOut) -> str {
    unsafe {
        let buf: []u8 = slice(u8, lo.p.*.raw, lo.p.*.cap)
        return sink.view(lo.p.*.s, buf)
    }
}

// Records the logger shipped — `log.emitted` on the logger it holds.
pub fn log_records(read lo: LogOut) -> i64 {
    unsafe { return log.emitted(lo.p.*.lg) }
}

// Frees the logger it took, and the destination.
pub fn free_log(mut lo: LogOut) {
    if lo.p == null { return }
    unsafe {
        log.free(lo.p.*.lg)
        free_ptr(lo.p.*.raw)
    }
    free_ptr(lo.p as *mut u8)
    lo.p = null
}

fn log_open(ctx: *mut u8, id: i64, parent: i64, name: str, start: i64, dur: i64) {
    var l: *mut LogState = ctx as *mut LogState
    unsafe {
        if log.begin(l.*.lg, l.*.level, name) {
            log.kv_i64(l.*.lg, "id", id)
            log.kv_i64(l.*.lg, "parent", parent)
            log.kv_i64(l.*.lg, "dur", dur)
        }
    }
}

fn log_str(ctx: *mut u8, key: str, value: str) {
    var l: *mut LogState = ctx as *mut LogState
    unsafe { log.kv_str(l.*.lg, key, value) }
}

fn log_i64(ctx: *mut u8, key: str, value: i64) {
    var l: *mut LogState = ctx as *mut LogState
    unsafe { log.kv_i64(l.*.lg, key, value) }
}

fn log_close(ctx: *mut u8) {
    var l: *mut LogState = ctx as *mut LogState
    unsafe {
        var buf: []u8 = slice(u8, l.*.raw, l.*.cap)
        log.end(l.*.lg, l.*.s, buf)
    }
}
